md/REFERENCE -- the two machine descriptions, and where their values come from
==============================================================================

This file describes the two Machine Descriptions (MD) of the firmware.
They are the "hypervisor MD" (hv) and the "guest MD" (md).
For every node and property it says:
    what defines it,
    who reads it,
    where its value comes from.

Layout
    hv   md/common.hdesc            macros and constants only
         md/legion/1up.hdesc        the legion machine, complete
         md/pci/1up.hdesc           the pci machine, complete
         -> 1up-hv.bin

    md   md/common.pdesc            macros and constants only
         md/legion/1up.pdesc        the legion machine, complete
         md/pci/1up.pdesc           the pci machine, complete
         -> 1up-md.bin

A build uses one machine, legion or pci, with the common.* files.

There are two parts:
    Part 1   the hypervisor MD   md/common.hdesc and md/*/1up.hdesc
    Part 2   the guest MD        md/common.pdesc and md/*/1up.pdesc

The values of the hypervisor MD are also commented in the .hdesc files.
The tables here are checked against the built MD files by
tests/full/md/internal/refdoc.sh (it runs tests/full/md/lib/refdoc.py).
Nothing in this file changes the MDs.


Sources
-------

The two Sun archives have short names, given with their download below:
"T1-archive" and "T2-archive".
A file is cited as <archive>:<path inside the archive>.
The sha256 sums of the archives are in
tests/full/md/expected/sun-archives.sha256.

"T1-archive" = OpenSPARCT1_Arch.1.5.tar.bz2
    http://download.oracle.com/technetwork/systems/opensparc/OpenSPARCT1_Arch.1.5.tar.bz2
    There are two kinds of files we use.

    1. MD source files:
         legion/src/config/niagara/{common,1up,1g2p}.hdesc and *.pdesc
         legion/common.hdesc
         hypervisor/src/greatlakes/ontario/t1_fpga/configs/*
       The hypervisor MD in these files has another layout than ours (one flat
       guest node).
       Only the property names and values are shared.

    2. The machine configuration legion/src/config/niagara/1up.conf.
       It has the address plan of the simulated machine.
       QEMU's niagara machine has the same addresses (the last column):
         1up.conf      address            QEMU niagara.c
         ------------- ------------------ --------------------------------------
         memory        0x80000000         NIAGARA_PARTITION_RAM_BASE
         disk          0x1f40000000       NIAGARA_VDISK_BASE
         nvram         0x1f11000000       NIAGARA_NVRAM_BASE
         guest MD      0x1f12000000       NIAGARA_MD_ROM_BASE
         hypervisor MD 0x1f12080000       NIAGARA_HV_ROM_BASE
         boot ROM      0xfff0000000       NIAGARA_PROM_BASE (reset.bin)
         q.bin         boot ROM + 0x10000 NIAGARA_PROM_BASE + NIAGARA_Q_OFFSET
         openboot.bin  boot ROM + 0x80000 NIAGARA_PROM_BASE + NIAGARA_OBP_OFFSET

"T2-archive" = OpenSPARCT2_Arch.1.2.tar.bz2
    https://download.oracle.com/technetwork/systems/opensparc/OpenSPARCT2_Arch.1.2.tar.bz2
    We use two things from it.

    1. The hypervisor code: hypervisor/src/common/src and
       hypervisor/src/greatlakes (BSD+).
       In this repository it is src/hypervisor/src. The "read at" columns point
       into it.
       No specification describes the hypervisor MD.
       So this code, which parses the MD, is the only first-hand source (see
       md/README).

    2. MD source files: t1_fpga/src/xilinx/configs/*_hv.desc and *_guest.desc.
       We call them "configs/" below.
       The constants are in
       t1_fpga/src/xilinx/include/xilinx_t1_system_config.h.
       No licence is named for these files. Their header says "Use is subject to
       license terms".
       Our .hdesc files have the layout of the *_hv.desc files.

QEMU
    hw/sparc64/niagara.c, the NIAGARA_* constants.
    We cite them where a value is an address of the machine.

Guest OS (called "OS" below)
    OpenSolaris dev b134 (osol-dev-134-ai-sparc.iso, 291942400 bytes,
    sha256 fe36ae8d3aea3797a10607c0e024c02fe91f99f43f64e5234280b7a0c4e8ab0a)
    is the OS whose code we read, to see how OS, the hypervisor and OpenBoot
    work together. We read it in illumos-gate, commit eb9dbf0cbc (2010-03-16),
    the last one before the ISO:
    git clone https://github.com/illumos/illumos-gate
    Cited as OS/<path under usr/src/uts>.
    The tests boot Solaris 10, whose kernel is closed: legion with the
    OpenSPARC T1 image, pci with S10u11.

Specifications
    The guest MD is described by Hypervisor API 2.0b and FWARC 2005/115.
    They are named at the start of part 2.


Where the "read at" files are
-----------------------------

A file name in a "read at" column has no directory when the file is in
hypervisor/src/common/src.
For the other files:
    config.h, guest.h, vdev_intr.h    hypervisor/src/greatlakes/common/include
    vdev_simdisk.s                    hypervisor/src/greatlakes/common/src
    main.s                            hypervisor/src/greatlakes/ontario/src and
                                      huron/src


Part 1. The hypervisor MD
=========================

The hypervisor MD is md/common.hdesc and md/*/1up.hdesc, built to 1up-hv.bin.
The hypervisor reads it. OpenBoot and OS do not.
No specification describes it.
The first-hand source is the hypervisor code that parses it: src/hypervisor/src
in this tree.

md/common.hdesc
---------------
It is included by every md/*/1up.hdesc and has macros and constants only: no
node and no property of its own.
The 1up.hdesc files use five of them.
The last column is hw/sparc64/niagara.c of QEMU, the machine these values must
fit:
    macro    value of value        QEMU niagara.c
    -------- -------- ------------ ---------------------------------------
    HVUART   hvuart   0xfff0c2c000 not in niagara.c;
                                   devel/0003-niagara-hvuart-console.patch of
                                   github.com/unix0cc/qemu-experimental-patches
                                   maps the UART there
    HVTOD    tod      0xfff0c1fff8 NIAGARA_RTC_BASE
    PUART(n) uartbase 0x1f10000000 NIAGARA_UART_BASE
                                   (used once, as PUART(0))
    ROMBASE  rombase  0xfff0080000 NIAGARA_PROM_BASE
                                   + NIAGARA_OBP_OFFSET
    ROMSIZE  romsize  512k         no counterpart: the PROM region
                                   is 4 MiB, openboot.bin is about 348 KB

Legend
------
    column     value     meaning
    ---------- --------- -----------------------------------------
    required   yes       parser reports an error if missing
               default 0 the hypervisor takes 0
               unread    no code in src/hypervisor reads it
               pci only  node is in the pci MD only
    read at    file:line in src/hypervisor/src
                         (no directory: hypervisor/src/common/src)
    T1-archive file      file of the archive that has it
    T2-archive -         the archive does not have it
"Required" is read in the code cited.
It was not tried by removing each property and booting.

