Part 2. The guest MD
====================

The guest MD is md/common.pdesc and md/*/1up.pdesc.
OpenBoot and OS read it. The hypervisor does not.
Unlike the hypervisor MD, it has published specifications:

    Hypervisor API 2.0b
    https://sun4v.github.io/downloads/Hypervisor-api-2.0b.pdf
                          chapter 8 (the machine description nodes)
    FWARC 2005/115        "sun4v machine description"
                          https://sun4v.github.io/ARChive/FWARC/2005/115/
                          md-bindings.pdf, section 5

Our pdesc files are Sun's files, headers kept, with our changes.
Most of their content lines are identical to the T1-archive files, far fewer to
the T2-archive files.

Legend
------
The table has one row per node and one per property of a node.
In this file a row is called a position.

    column              value      meaning
    ------------------- ---------- -------------------------------------
    Hypervisor API 2.0b 8.x.y      section that lists the position
    FWARC 2005/115      R, O       required, optional, as in the spec
                        -          not in that table (see below)
    in T1, in T2        y          name in the pdesc files, same node
                        o          name in another node
                        -          name nowhere
    read y/n            y          OpenBoot or OS reads it
                        n          nothing found (see "The read column" below)
    where               OBP/#N     reader; #N is the note below
                                   OBP  OpenBoot, src/obp/obp/arch/sun4v
                                   OS   OS source, usr/src/uts
                                   API  the Hypervisor API
                                   none nothing found that reads it

    T1 files: T1-archive legion/src/config/niagara/*.pdesc
              T1-archive hypervisor/src/greatlakes/ontario/t1_fpga/configs/
              *.pdesc
    T2 files: T2-archive t1_fpga/src/xilinx/configs/*_guest.desc (no licence
    named)
    T1 and T2 test the name, not the value or the line.
    OpenBoot reads nodes that no specification table here lists:
        the nodes iodevice and interrupt-map-entry (FWARC 2007/386 and the cases
        around it; for pci see md/pci/README and
        "The iodevice values" at the end)
        the nodes openboot, devalias and phys_io (OpenBoot's own)
    A note names the file of the reader, without line numbers.

The read column
    Each position was looked up in the source code, and each hit read in its
    context, to see which MD node it asks. The readers found are in the "where"
    column and the notes. A reader that builds the name at run time would be
    missed.

