The iodevice values
    Where the values of the pci iodevice node (md/pci) come
    from; the readers are in the notes above.
    The hypervisor (src/hypervisor/src/greatlakes: ontario/include/fire.h,
    ontario/include/fire/fire.h, ontario/src/vpci_fire.s, huron/include/piu.h,
    huron/include/piu/piu.h):
        address-ranges       three bases and sizes (IO, MEM32, MEM64); the last
                             64 KB of MEM32 and MEM64 are the MSI windows, so
                             those two are shorter by that
        virtual-dma          the 2 GB DVMA window
        msi-address-ranges   MSI32 0x7fff0000 and MSI64 0x3ffff0000, 64 KB each
        #msi, msi-ranges     FIRE_MAX_MSIS, 256
        #msi-eqs             FIRE_NEQS, 36
        msi-eq-to-devino     the 36 queues from devino 0x18 (FIRE_EQ2INO)
        msi-eq-size          FIRE_NEQRECORDS, 128
        interrupts           0x3f, 0x3e: the error mondos 62 and 63
        cfg-handle           pcie_bus.cfghandle of md/pci/1up.hdesc
    FWARC 2007/386 and the code that reads them (OBP, px): device-type,
    compatible, #interrupt-cells, msix-data-width (px enables MSI-X for 32),
    disabled (0: OBP probes the node).
    The interrupt-map-entry nodes: parent-interrupt 0x14..0x16 are the INOs of
    INTA..INTD in the table of interrupt numbers of the OpenSPARC T2 SoC
    Microarchitecture Specification (volume 2: INO 20-23 INTx, 24-59 event
    queues, 62 and 63 internal); child-unit-address and child-interrupt follow
    the PCI binding and FWARC 2007/386; parent-device-path is /pci@ plus the
    cfg-handle.
    Ours (md/pci/README): probe-list, interrupt-map-mask, bus-ranges.
    No code needs msi-data-mask: note 15.
